        "contextualSenseReviewed": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "word-que--clause-link",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "que",
      "answerEn": "that; links an expression to its content or an outcome",
      "noteEn": "Que can introduce what someone thinks, says, wants, causes, or allows, or what an evaluation is about. English may use 'that,' omit it, or reorganize the sentence with 'to' or 'for.' Que stays unaccented; the following verb form depends on the surrounding expression.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Creo que Ana viene.",
        "en": "I think that Ana is coming."
      },
      "tags": [
        "word",
        "contextual sense"
      ],
      "reviewStatus": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
      "schedulable": true,
      "provenance": {
        "method": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
        "ownerComicId": "seed-curriculum",
        "contextualSenseReviewed": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "word-tengas--possibility-subjunctive",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "tengas",
      "answerEn": "you have (a special form of tener)",
      "noteEn": "Tengas is a form of tener, “to have,” for tú, one familiar person. Certain surrounding expressions require tengas instead of the ordinary tienes: puede que tengas presents a possibility, and es bueno que tengas gives an evaluation.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Puede que tengas otra llave.",
        "en": "You may have another key."
      },
      "tags": [
        "word",
        "tener",
        "possibility",
        "subjunctive",
        "A2"
      ],
      "reviewStatus": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
      "schedulable": true,
      "provenance": {
        "method": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
        "ownerComicId": "es-xkcd-convincente",
        "contextualSenseReviewed": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-von-neumann-self-replicating-machine",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "máquina autorreplicante de Von Neumann",
      "answerEn": "a theoretical machine that can construct another machine from a description and pass that description to the result",
      "noteEn": "John von Neumann's self-reproduction model combines a universal constructor with a copied description of the machine. The comic loosely treats the human reproductive system as an internal ‘factory’ that makes more people; humans are not literal copies of one another or implementations of the formal machine.",
      "tags": [
        "concept",
